WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.860
Speaker 1: You know, I've gotten a lot of emails&nbsp;
over the last month or so from people saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:11.640
David, sir, I hear it vaguely mentioned that the&nbsp;
Republican Party is now more extreme than ever.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:17.760
But is that true? Is it true that the Republican&nbsp;
Party has actually moved to the right, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:22.440
particularly in the context of looking at people&nbsp;
like Marjorie Taylor GREENE and Lauren Boebert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:28.620
and similar extremist lunatics in the Republican&nbsp;
Party? It's reasonable to ask, has it always been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:34.140
like this? And the truth is that it has not. This&nbsp;
is not a segment about how great the Republican&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:39.660
Party used to be. This is a segment about how&nbsp;
much less extreme the Republican Party used to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:44.400
be. For those who don't know the history, the&nbsp;
Republican Party was once a relatively moderate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:49.560
political force in the United States compared&nbsp;
to where they are today. Over the last 40 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:54.540
there has been a radical transformation in the&nbsp;
Republican Party, where it has become more and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:59.520
more extreme in its views and its policies,&nbsp;
in its rhetoric and in the level of extremism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:05.220
that it is willing to accept within its ranks.&nbsp;
Again, I might have to say this 20 times. I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:10.860
not rehabilitating the old Republican Party. I'm&nbsp;
comparing what it used to be to what it is today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:15.900
And one of the most glaring sort of examples of&nbsp;
this shift has been the party's view on social&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:22.380
issues. In the seventies and eighties, there&nbsp;
were many elements of the Republican Party that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:28.380
were generally and sort of superficially at least&nbsp;
supportive of abortion rights and of gay rights.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:33.720
But we know that that has changed dramatically.&nbsp;
And it is a party that has won many elections and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:39.120
raised a ton of money being completely against&nbsp;
abortion and completely against LGBT rights.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:45.300
If you go to the seventies and eighties, you&nbsp;
can find that abortion rights and LGBT rights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:53.400
were simply not as Partizan as were not Partizan.&nbsp;
Issues to the degree that they are today. And you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:56.940
had some Republicans that were actually supportive&nbsp;
of those causes. If you look at President&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.940 --> 00:02:02.820
Richard Nixon, one of the first politicians to&nbsp;
publicly support abortion rights in a 69 speech,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:07.920
he said this should be a decision made by a woman&nbsp;
and her doctor, not the government. That's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:14.880
small government position on abortion. In 72,&nbsp;
the Republican Party platform included a plank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:21.180
supporting abortion rights to some degree. They&nbsp;
maintained that for four years, and in 1976,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:26.400
the Republican Party became significantly&nbsp;
more anti-abortion. You go to 1977,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:32.880
a Republican led Congress passed a bill known as&nbsp;
the gay rights bill, and it banned discrimination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:36.660
on the basis of sexual orientation in&nbsp;
the federal government and in Washington,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:44.940
D.C. That was a Republican led Congress at the&nbsp;
time. You then look at Ronald Reagan in 86 when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:51.600
Reagan signed the Immigration Reform and Control&nbsp;
Act, it actually provided some legal protections&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:58.440
for gay and lesbian immigrants to the United&nbsp;
States in 87. The Republican Party platform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:05.040
included language supporting anti-discrimination&nbsp;
measures for gay and lesbian Americans. So it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:09.540
not that those views necessarily reflected the&nbsp;
views of the entire Republican Party at those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:15.300
points in time. But there used to be support,&nbsp;
at least to some degree, for those causes among&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:22.500
Republicans in the seventies and eighties, so that&nbsp;
social issues, taxation. In the past, Republicans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:29.640
were open to the idea that we might raise taxes&nbsp;
if it meant funding important programs or if it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:37.020
would reduce the deficit. Today, they never even&nbsp;
acknowledge that increasing taxes to fund certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:40.920
programs can actually reduce the deficit by&nbsp;
being economically stimulative. They pretend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:46.740
that that's not an economic reality anymore, and&nbsp;
we know the result. It's led to the erosion of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:52.500
so many different social programs. Immigration&nbsp;
is an area where the views of the Republicans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:59.760
have changed quite dramatically. In the past,&nbsp;
Republicans were often supportive of immigration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:06.300
saw it as a source of economic growth, sort&nbsp;
of rock with the cultural diversity component&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:12.660
of it as well. But now we've seen the Republican&nbsp;
Party become obsessed with building a border wall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:17.220
cracking down on immigration, saying we shouldn't&nbsp;
even provide a path to citizenship to people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:21.720
were brought here as kids by their parents through&nbsp;
no fault of their own. But when you go back to 86,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:27.840
I mentioned that immigration reform act that&nbsp;
President Reagan signed, it provided amnesty to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:32.520
millions of undocumented immigrants already living&nbsp;
in the United States. And Reagan said at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:39.840
quote, The legalization provisions in this act&nbsp;
will go far to improve the lives of a class&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:46.320
of individuals who now must hide in the shadows&nbsp;
without access to many of the benefits of a free&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:54.360
and open society. That was Reagan who said that&nbsp;
John McCain in 2005 co-sponsored the Comprehensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.360 --> 00:05:00.540
Immigration Reform Act, which would have provided&nbsp;
a path to citizenship for undocumented immigrants.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:07.440
Mitt Romney, as recently as 2012, expressed&nbsp;
support for comprehensive immigration reform,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:13.980
which would have given a path to citizenship for&nbsp;
some undocumented immigrants. So this has been a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:21.180
change. Climate change. I'll mention briefly you&nbsp;
had a time in the past when Republicans were open&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:27.180
to talking about what actions should we take&nbsp;
to address the issue of climate change. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:31.080
didn't necessarily agree with Democrats&nbsp;
on what those actions actions should be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:37.800
but they agreed on the science. They didn't&nbsp;
question the science. You now have much of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:43.080
the Republican Party that just says, Oh, no, the&nbsp;
science is completely wrong. So the Republican&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:48.720
Party's shift to the extreme right is very&nbsp;
real. It's been extraordinarily impactful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:53.760
because it has influenced media coverage of&nbsp;
a lot of these issues. Media coverage has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:58.860
influenced public opinion on a lot of these&nbsp;
issues. But the Republican Party has moved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:04.920
dramatically to the extreme. And I don't know&nbsp;
at this point what's going to turn it around.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:10.758
Let me know your thoughts. Leave a comment.&nbsp;
Send an email to info at David Pakman dot com.

